    "created_at": "2026-10-30T14:44:54Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-30T14:44:54Z",
    "body": "The concern is valid for:\n\n* Legacy serial connectors (RS-232 serial ports and similar)\n* USB-to-serial adapters (or Bluetooth-to-serial, or any transport-to-serial) that allow connecting arbitrary serial devices through the same adapter\n\nAs noted, many serial devices are USB devices with built-in serial adapters and typically have non-generic vendor/product IDs. Browsers can match against these identifiers to implement a blocklist.\n\nSome types of Bluetooth serial devices are supported through WebSerial implementations:\n\n* Bluetooth [Nordic UART Service (NUS)](https://nrfconnectdocs.nordicsemi.com/ncs/latest/nrf/libraries/bluetooth/services/nus.html)\n* Bluetooth [Serial Port Profile (SPP)](https://www.bluetooth.com/specifications/specs/serial-port-profile-1-1/)\n* Bluetooth [RFCOMM](https://www.bluetooth.com/specifications/specs/rfcomm-1-1/) services\n\nWhen the serial port is part of a Bluetooth device, the user agent can rely on Bluetooth identifiers to determine which device is connected and potentially block it. Additionally, if it is a RFCOMM service then it has a service UUID which can be blocklisted.",
    "author_association": "COLLABORATOR",
    "pin": null,
